 53.429, 53.499, 53.569, 53.65, 53.743, 53.824, 53.952, 53.987, 54.033, 54.056, 54.091, 54.126, 54.149, 54.184, 54.219, 54.254, 54.3, 54.335, 54.358, 54.439, 54.486, 54.579, 54.625, 54.707, 54.776, 54.846, 54.869, 54.892, 54.915, 54.997, 55.043, 55.206, 55.287, 55.322, 55.45, 55.577, 55.809, 55.891, 55.96, 56.042, 56.076, 56.123, 56.158, 56.193, 56.227, 56.274, 56.32, 56.402, 56.529, 56.599, 56.645, 56.68, 56.761, 56.889, 56.959, 57.029, 57.063, 57.11, 57.133, 57.156, 57.179, 57.214, 57.249, 57.377, 57.47, 57.609, 57.806, 58.294, 58.387, 58.468, 58.549, 58.584, 58.607, 58.689, 58.735, 58.816, 58.898, 58.991, 59.118, 59.316, 59.989, 60.117, 60.186, 60.233, 60.279, 60.349, 60.419, 60.465, 60.488, 60.558, 60.628, 60.697, 60.767, 60.837, 60.883, 60.964, 61.046, 61.15, 61.278, 61.406, 61.499, 61.58, 61.673, 61.696, 61.719, 61.812, 61.882, 61.951, 62.021, 62.091, 62.184, 62.23, 62.323, 62.416, 62.462, 62.532, 62.578, 62.625, 62.671, 62.776, 62.869, 62.938, 62.961, 62.985, 63.031, 63.066, 63.101, 63.136, 63.217, 63.287, 63.426, 63.554, 63.681, 63.809, 63.89, 63.96, 63.995, 64.064, 64.111, 64.146, 64.192, 64.215, 64.285, 64.355, 64.552, 64.633, 64.796, 64.842, 65.202, 65.295, 65.423, 65.492, 65.539, 65.562, 65.609, 65.655, 65.701, 65.748, 65.783, 65.818, 65.864, 65.887, 65.91, 65.934, 66.003, 66.05, 66.154, 66.259, 66.386, 66.433, 66.561, 66.688, 66.886, 66.955, 67.025, 67.06, 67.095, 67.129, 67.164, 67.199, 67.234, 67.269, 67.304, 67.338, 67.373, 67.408, 67.431, 67.466, 67.501, 67.571, 67.698, 67.78, 67.907, 68.035, 68.36]}, "text": "Homer tells it in Book Nine of the Odyssey, and he lets Odysseus tell it himself. So this is the hero's own account. They reach the land of the Cyclopes, a place Homer never locates. On a cliff stands a cave, full of sheep and goats. Odysseus takes twelve men to look. The owner is out. His men find racks of cheese, and suggest taking some and leaving. Odysseus refuses. He wants to meet the owner, in case there is a gift. The owner is Pollifeemus. Homer says he looks less like a man who eats bread, than like a wooded peak, standing alone above the others. He drives his flock inside, and rolls a stone across the entrance. Homer says twenty-two four-wheeled wagons could not have moved it. That stone is the real trap. Odysseus thinks of drawing his sword, then realises that if he kills the giant, no one can ever open the door. So he waits. Across three meals, Pollifeemus eats six of his men, two at a time. Homer gives the detail. We will leave it out.", "orig": "Homer tells it in Book Nine of the Odyssey, and he lets Odysseus tell it himself. So this is the hero's own account. They reach the land of the Cyclopes, a place Homer never locates. On a cliff stands a cave, full of sheep and goats. Odysseus takes twelve men to look. The owner is out. His men find racks of cheese, and suggest taking some and leaving. Odysseus refuses. He wants to meet the owner, in case there is a gift. The owner is Polyphemus. Homer says he looks less like a man who eats bread, than like a wooded peak, standing alone above the others. He drives his flock inside, and rolls a stone across the entrance. Homer says twenty-two four-wheeled wagons could not have moved it. That stone is the real trap. Odysseus thinks of drawing his sword, then realises that if he kills the giant, no one can ever open the door. So he waits. Across three meals, Polyphemus eats six of his men, two at a time. Homer gives the detail. We will leave it out."}